Radix's mounts and filesystems

Radix lists its mounts in /processes/mounts, as "source /path kind rw|ro",
with the filesystem's label after them if it has one, escaping a space,
tab, newline or backslash in a field as Linux's /proc/mounts does. Sizes
come from statvfs(3), in fragments. A filesystem is special when no disk is
behind it, and a network share is served through FUSE, so it is FUSE's.
A mount statvfs fails on is left out with a warning, as on Linux.

diff --git a/filesystems_radix.go b/filesystems_radix.go
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..d277a3e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/filesystems_radix.go
@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@
+//go:build radix
+// +build radix
+
+package main
+
+// Radix serves a network share through FUSE and has no filesystem of its own
+// for one, so a share is reported by isFuseFs.
+func isNetworkFs(m Mount) bool {
+	return false
+}
+
+func isFuseFs(m Mount) bool {
+	return m.Fstype == "fuse"
+}
+
+// The special filesystems are the ones with no disk behind them: memory files,
+// the kernel's views of itself and its devices, and the boot archive.
+func isSpecialFs(m Mount) bool {
+	switch m.Fstype {
+	case "tmpfs", "proc", "sys", "dev", "shm", "pipefs", "bootfs":
+		return true
+	}
+
+	return false
+}
+
+func isHiddenFs(m Mount) bool {
+	return false
+}
